---
import Entry from "../../layouts/Entry.astro";
import { url } from "../../lib/site";
---

<Entry
  title="Newsletter privacy"
  eyebrow="The Charitology Letter"
  summary="What subscribing shares, how confirmation works and how to stop receiving the letter."
  crumbs={[
    { label: "The Charitology Letter", href: "/newsletter/" },
    { label: "Privacy" },
  ]}
>
  <h2>Your choice</h2><p>
    We ask for an email address and explicit newsletter consent. A confirmation
    email verifies that you control the inbox. A request alone does not join the
    newsletter. Opening a confirmation page alone does not subscribe you;
    confirmation requires a deliberate action.
  </p>
  <h2>What the mail service keeps</h2><p>
    When email sign-up is available, the mail service keeps your address,
    subscription state, consent version and request/confirmation times.
    Confirmation credentials expire and are stored as hashes. Limited request
    records support abuse prevention. Delivery events help us handle failed
    addresses, complaints and unsubscribe requests.
  </p>
  <p>
    The website is hosted on GitHub Pages. The separate mail service uses
    Vercel, a private Neon database and Resend to process subscriptions and
    deliver emails. These providers process the data necessary for those
    functions under their service agreements. Their privacy notices describe
    their own practices: <a href="https://vercel.com/legal/privacy-notice">Vercel</a>,
    <a href="https://neon.com/privacy-policy">Neon</a> and
    <a href="https://resend.com/legal/privacy-policy">Resend</a>. Subscriber
    records are not published in the website, public API, search index or GitHub
    repository.
  </p>
  <h2>What we use it for</h2><p>
    Your newsletter address is for The Charitology Letter and messages needed to
    manage that subscription. We do not sell it, publish it or add it to a donor
    or volunteer list. Subscribing does not authorize another program to contact
    you. We do not use tracking pixels or tracked links in the letter.
  </p>
  <h2>Unsubscribe and requests</h2><p>
    Use the unsubscribe or preferences link in a letter to stop receiving it.
    Minimal suppression information may remain to prevent accidental
    resubscription. For an access, correction or deletion request, email <a
      href="mailto:hello@charitology.org">hello@charitology.org</a
    > from the subscribed address. We may need to verify control before disclosing
    or changing a record.
  </p>
  <p>
    If your address is already unsubscribed across the shared Resend account, a
    Charitology signup must not silently reverse that preference. The
    confirmation flow will explain any additional step needed.
  </p>
  <h2>Keep reading without subscribing</h2><p>
    All editions remain available on the website and through the <a
      href={url("/newsletter.xml")}>newsletter RSS feed</a
    >. Read <a href={url("/newsletter/")}>The Charitology Letter</a>.
  </p>
</Entry>
